Z||000001||RZURCTN||11-28-1999||11:18 PM||||I guess you'll never have to worry about running out of gas!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Sorry, I have no clue what could be wrong with it.  I do have an extra fuel guage if you need one!||||reg||1
Z||000002||mutfish||11-29-1999||06:38 AM||||I had the same problem with my fuel gage when I bought my fiero.I finally had to drop the tank,remove the pump/sending unit assembly and clean the resistor on the sender.I also replcaced the pump while the tank was down.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Linx||11-29-1999||01:32 PM||||Man- Ive got a dollar here that says it's your gauge sender in the tank.  At the heart of it is a wire-wound resister that'll open and give a false indication of way-past-full.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Bad thing is, it's part of the fuel pump and you've got to drop the tank.<P>To be sure tho', short the pins on the fuel pump harness connector that's against the fire wall. It's a 3 pin connector, of which one is ground and the other is the sender.  That'll give an indication of <I>empty</I>.  If your needle swings to the "E" its a safe bet the sender opened.||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||11-29-1999||05:31 PM||||My fuel gauge did a similar act. Around 3\4 of a tank, it would shoot to full untill I got past a 1\2 a tank.  Then it would work fine.  My fuel pump was making noise so I had it replaced and it solved both problems.  It cost about 280.00 to have it done.  Yes the tank does need to be dropped.||||reg||1
